Superficial puncture wound from stepping on nail (through shoe) to sole of foot
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Plantar circular defect, Mechanism of puncture through footwear, Pain localized to wound tract

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-035
Reason for Visit: Acute puncture wound to the sole of the foot requiring urgent assessment for retained foreign bodies, deep space infection, and tetanus prophylaxis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a puncture injury through footwear, highly contaminating the wound tract. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to thoroughly probe and irrigate the wound, assess for deep structural compromise, and initiate prophylactic antibiotics to prevent devastating plantar space infections (including Pseudomonas) before morning clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I stepped on a nail that went right through my shoe into my foot, and now it hurts sharply when I stand.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a clear history of stepping on an upright sharp object (nail), specifically penetrating through footwear (sneaker) 2 hours ago. This resulted in a small, highly localized circular epidermal defect on the plantar surface of the foot. The patient reports sharp, penetrating pain directly related to weight-bearing or direct palpation of the tract. There is minimal to no surface bleeding due to the natural retraction of the thick plantar skin. There is mild surrounding erythema that has not rapidly expanded in the immediate acute phase.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a severe limp; strictly avoiding heel-to-toe weight transfer.
Social/Safety Concerns: Discarded construction materials in the yard pose an ongoing hazard.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/80 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable when seated, visibly distressed when forced to apply weight to the affected foot.
Physical Exam: Plantar examination reveals a small circular epidermal defect. Tenderness is isolated to the puncture tract without generalized deep compartment pain. Visual inspection and bedside probing confirm an absence of a visible retained foreign body protruding from the puncture site. Normal active and passive range of motion of the toes and midfoot is present without deep pain exacerbation. Intact sensation and normal capillary refill are noted in all digits distal to the puncture.
Environmental Observations: Rusted nail on a board identified outside; patient advised to dispose of it safely.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial puncture wound to sole of foot - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Retained foreign body (shoe rubber/metal) - Ruled out due to: Thorough visual and tactile exploration yielding no superficial material; however, definitive clearance requires plain radiography.

Deep plantar space infection / Pseudomonas osteochondritis - Ruled out due to: Acute presentation (<2 hours) with complete absence of massive foot swelling, fluctuance, or systemic fever.

Flexor tendon laceration - Ruled out due to: Intact, strong, and completely pain-free active flexion of the toes against resistance.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Puncture wounds through footwear carry an exceptionally high risk of introducing Pseudomonas aeruginosa and shoe rubber deep into the plantar fascia. Bedside evaluation definitively cleared major tendon involvement and allowed for immediate high-pressure irrigation of the tract. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to execute necessary localized decontamination, update tetanus status, and prescribe immediate prophylactic antibiotics to avert severe, fast-moving deep space infections, safely bridging the patient to daytime radiographic clearance.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered local anesthesia to allow for high-pressure syringe irrigation of the puncture tract with sterile saline. Applied a non-adherent dressing. Administered Tetanus booster. Prescribed a 5-day course of oral antibiotics (e.g., Cephalexin or Ciprofloxacin based on local guidelines) to cover typical skin flora and Pseudomonas from the footwear.
Positive Behavioral Change: Use crutches or strictly heel-walk for the next 48 hours to entirely offload the puncture site. Preventing body weight from compressing the wound stops the deep tissue layers from grinding together, severely reducing pain and preventing any microscopic bacteria from being pushed deeper into the foot.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Crutches.
Follow-up: Routine GP review tomorrow with standard anteroposterior, lateral, and oblique foot radiographs to definitively exclude retained radiopaque metallic fragments or shoe rubber.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if the foot becomes massively swollen, intensely red, hot to the touch, or if you develop an unmanageable throbbing pain, fever, or chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Soft materials like shoe rubber cannot always be seen on x-rays or found clinically; close monitoring for infection is essential.
Clinical Course: Sharp pain upon standing will persist for 2 to 4 days. The puncture site will seal quickly, but the deep tissues heal slowly over 1 to 2 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if a red, tender lump develops under the skin, or if red streaks begin traveling up your ankle.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A nail pushes dirty rubber from the shoe deep into the foot. The antibiotics kill this specific bacteria, and washing the wound removes the immediate dirt.
Actionable Steps: Take your antibiotics exactly as prescribed. Get the foot x-ray tomorrow. Do not walk barefoot, and do not put your full body weight on the injured spot.

